{
  "id": "Compound::DB00376 - Gene::1133",
  "verbalization": "Trihexyphenidyl, a muscarinic receptor antagonist, is shown to bind to Chrm4 and Chrm2, both of which participate in G-protein coupled receptor activities. These activities are ultimately linked to Chrm5, suggesting a potential mechanism by which Trihexyphenidyl influences the function of muscarinic receptors. The evidence indicates that Trihexyphenidyl may modulate the activity of Chrm5 through its interaction with Chrm4 or Chrm2, which are both involved in the same biological pathway. The overlapping participation in G-protein coupled receptor activities implies a shared functional role in cellular signaling, potentially allowing Trihexyphenidyl to exert its effects on Chrm5 via indirect pathways. The integration of these paths highlights a possible dependency on muscarinic receptor signaling, with Trihexyphenidyl acting as a modulator of receptor activity through its interaction with these receptors.",
